103
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:57,940
Now this may be news to some but not all neonatal and infant airway people use stilets.

104
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:01,940
There is some concern that the rigid stilets can cause damage to the airway.

105
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:06,760
There is also concern that the plastic at the distal tip of the stilets can shear off

106
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:08,880
into the airway.

107
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:14,160
Because of this, most guidelines for neonatal or infant airway management mention the stilets

108
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:16,560
as an optional piece of equipment.

109
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:22,160
There was a 2017 Cochrane review in the infant population and then a 2021 study on the neonatal

110
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:27,240
population, both of which compared the use of a stilets with a no-stilets approach and

111
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:31,880
neither study could confirm that stilets use yields better outcomes.

112
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:35,900
The takeaway here is that stilets use with the neonatal or infant is at the provider's

113
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:41,180
discretion, unless for those of us in the EMS field we have more specific instruction

114
00:07:41,180 --> 00:07:44,260
provided to us by medical direction.

115
00:07:44,260 --> 00:07:48,560
That said, there are some potential advantages to using a stilets and therefore a bougie

116
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:52,140
as well for these patient populations.

117
00:07:52,140 --> 00:07:56,980
One idea is that the stilets allow the clinician to shape a bend in the end of the tube.

118
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,800
A shallow angle is often preferred with younger patients, while a more defined hockey stick

119
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:05,480
shape is often the norm for adult patients.

120
00:08:05,480 --> 00:08:10,560
Given that nearly all ET tubes come packaged with a notable bend in them, using a tool

121
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:16,400
to reshape them into a shallower angle, i.e. a stilet, or using a tool that presents with

122
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:20,920
a shallower angle, i.e. a bougie, may make more sense.

123
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:23,240
And what about the bougie specifically?

124
00:08:23,260 --> 00:08:28,940
There was a 2015 simulation study that showed promise of bougie use in shortening the time

125
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:34,060
to a definitive airway, as well as success in some difficult airway scenarios.

126
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:39,620
And note that this was specific to infants and done in simulation on mannequins.

127
00:08:39,620 --> 00:08:44,980
That said, progress has been slow and confounded by case studies of complications related to

128
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:46,440
